WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.600
"The Healthiest Food
Sources of Vitamin B12"

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:16.270
A regular, reliable source of vitamin
B12 is critical for anyone eating

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:18.670
a plant-based diet, either
vitamin B12 supplements

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:21.430
or vitamin B12 fortified foods.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:24.470
I've talked about my B12
supplement recommendations,

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:29.400
either 50 micrograms a day, or once
a week doses of 2,000 micrograms.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.810
I think that's the simplest, cheapest
way: taking it once a week.

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:36.300
That's how I do it. But if you
don't want to take supplements,

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.219
you'd have to rely on B12-fortified
foods, in which case you'd have

00:00:40.219 --> 00:00:43.309
to eat three separate servings
of B12-fortified foods,

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:49.249
each ideally containing at least
190% of the so-called "Daily Value"

00:00:49.249 --> 00:00:54.800
on the product's nutrition facts label.
How does that make any sense?

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.140
And what's the term "Daily Value"
even mean? The term Daily Value

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:04.140
is used to designate both the
DRV, Daily Reference Values,

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:07.430
and RDI, Reference Daily
Intakes, that are based on the

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:11.580
Recommended Daily Allowances,
all to limit consumer confusion.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:14.500
See, aren't you less confused now?

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.170
Anyway, the daily value of B12 was
set at 6, which was recently changed

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:22.800
to 2.4 in 2020, making it
all the more confusing,

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.020
and so 190% of 2.4
is about 4.5.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.320
Plug that into the equation
I detailed in the last video

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:33.380
and you get about 1.16
absorbed from each serving,

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:38.240
times three times a day, and poof:
there's your 3.5 for the day.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:42.470
OK, so how much like nutritional
yeast would that be,

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:47.100
a commonly B12-fortified food
source? It depends on the brand.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:53.540
Going alphabetically, Bob's Red Mill
brand has 730% per quarter cup,

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:57.170
so you'd only need about 4 times
less than that to make up a serving,

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:00.700
so around one tablespoon.
So one tablespoon of this brand

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:04.820
sprinkled on each meal and your
B12 would be taken care of.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:10.250
Bragg's says it's even more
potent at 563% per tablespoon;

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:13.100
so just a teaspoon three
times a day should suffice.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:19.800
Dr. Fuhrman's brand is explicitly
unfortified, and so contains zero B12,

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:24.300
so that's an important lesson. You can't
just assume nutritional yeast has B12.

00:02:24.319 --> 00:02:28.220
So if you find it in the bulk section;
I mean you have no idea

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:31.100
what it contains unless
you actually see the package.

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:35.260
Same with the Frontier Co-op
brand: zero vitamin B12.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:40.380
KAL brand has 500% of the Daily Value
of B12 per three rounded tablespoons,

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.840
so one rounded tablespoon should
suffice as one of the servings.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:48.550
NOW brand has more, with
two teaspoons sufficing.

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:53.980
Red Star has 333% per
one-and-a-half heaping tablespoons,

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.740
so a serving would be
like one tablespoon,

00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:00.210
but note only some of Red Star's
nutritional yeast varieties

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:04.100
have any B12 at all. So just
remember to check the label.

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:09.770
And finally, Trader Joe's looks like 1.5
tablespoons could count as one serving.

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:15.300
So it looks like Bragg's is the
most potent currently available.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:18.870
There are all sorts of other B12
fortified foods from plant-based

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:21.780
meats and milks to breakfast
cereals and energy drinks,

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:25.849
but are there any other green light
sources, meaning plant foods from

00:03:25.849 --> 00:03:29.989
which nothing bad has been added
and nothing good has been taken away?

00:03:29.989 --> 00:03:33.840
What about various algae-type
products like spirulina,

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:38.069
which are advertised as
natural vitamin B12 sources?

00:03:38.069 --> 00:03:42.910
Not only do they not actually contain
B12 that's useable for humans,

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:46.400
it's even worse than that. 
They may contain B12 analogues,

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:51.600
look-alike molecules that can even
block your absorption of real B12.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.800
I was excited to see that there
was an herbal tea with B12,

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:57.860
but so little you'd have
to quadruple bag it.

00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:02.280
If you didn't want to take a pill, which
again I think is really the best way,

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:05.840
the easiest option would
probably be LeafSide foods.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.340
I've always loved them because
they center their ingredients

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:12.800
around my Daily Dozen. Unfortunately,
people see them citing my science

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:16.519
and think I have some sort of financial
relationship, but of course I have

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:21.079
no financial ties to any food company,
drug company, supplements, kitchen

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:26.800
gadgets—no personal financial ties with
any commercial entity whatsoever, ever.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.980
I'm happy to voluntarily plug LeafSide
though, as their food has kept me

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:36.100
from starving on the road on many
occasion. They're freeze-dried,

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:39.340
so they're light and easy to travel
with, and I just use my hotel room

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:42.900
coffee maker to make
hot water and poof.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:47.300
But anyway, each of their meals
has 75 mcg of B12. So one a day

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:51.010
and you'd get all your B12 without
having to take a supplement,

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:57.260
but it's only 100% green light if you
specify you want the salt-free versions.

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:00.400
They have no added salt/fat/sugar
versions of all their products

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:05.680
at no extra cost, but you have
to specify that when you order.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:10.410
Note you have to throw all these
recommendations out the window

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:15.710
for anyone over age 65 and go straight
to high daily supplement doses,

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:18.350
which I'll cover in my next video,
as well as my recommendations

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:23.600
during pregnancy, breastfeeding,
infancy, and childhood.

